.\" tvchat(1) man page.  man(7) macros, like tvmail's.
.TH TVCHAT 1 "October 2026" "tvchat 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tvchat \- a Turbo Vision chat client (IRC and talk(1))
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tvchat
.RB [ \-\-debug [ FILE ] ]
.RB [ \-\-trace [ FILE ] ]
.RB [ \-\-help | \-\-version ]
.br
.B tvchat \-\-selftest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tvchat
is a chat client for the text terminal in the style of Borland's
.BR "Turbo Vision" ,
the sibling of
.BR tvmail (1).
Its one window has three panes:
the chat accounts (left), the highlighted account's conversations (top right,
starting with its server messages), and the conversation picked, its log over
an input line (bottom right).
.PP
.B tvchat
draws the windows only.
The connections, the protocols and the history are kept by
.BR tvchat\-backend (1),
which it runs as a co-process and polls for events while it idles.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Tab ", " Shift\-Tab
servers \(-> conversations \(-> input, and round.
.TP
.B Enter
on a server: connect it if need be and go to its conversations;
on a conversation: go to the input line;
in the input line: send.
.TP
.B PgUp ", " PgDn ", " Up ", " Down
scroll the log from the input line; PgUp at the top fetches older messages
(asking the server, when it keeps history).
.TP
.B Alt\-C
the Chat menu: Connect..., Disconnect..., Join... (a channel, a nick, an address
or an invite link), Invite..., Accept invite..., Leave, the Delta Chat group
items, and one item per account.
.B F5
reload
.RB ( "File \(-> Reload" ):
the windows re-read the backend's state.
.B F1
help.
.B F6
next window.
.B Alt\-0
window list.
.B Ctrl\-L
redraw the whole screen
.RB ( "Window \(-> Redraw screen" ).
.B Alt\-X
quit (asking first about text typed and not sent).
.SH TALK
An account with
.B protocol = talk
talks talk(1) through the LAN master's talkd.
Chat > Join... calls a user (or user@host); a call for you shows in the
status line and the conversation list, and Enter (or Chat > Answer) in it
answers; Chat > Leave hangs up or declines.
.TP
.B classic
talk's own screen in the chat pane: you on top, them below, every key sent
as typed (Backspace, Ctrl-U, Ctrl-W too).
.TP
.B IRC
the log: a line goes on Enter; \(lq... is typing\(rq shows while their line
is unfinished.
.PP
View > Talk view (Ctrl-T) switches the conversation shown; an account starts
in its
.B talk_view
(classic unless it says irc).
.SH "DELTA CHAT"
Encrypted chat over a mailbox of its own (\fBprotocol = deltachat\fR; needs the
official \fBdeltachat-rpc-server\fR core, see INSTALL.md and PRIMER.md).  Two
people swap keys once: Chat > Join... with an email address mails them an
invitation, and their tvchat asks \(lqAccept?\(rq (Chat > Accept invite...
asks again); Chat > Invite... shows an invite link to pass on by other means,
and Join also takes such a link.  A message from someone new waits as a
request; the first reply accepts it.
.PP
Chat > New group... makes an encrypted group (people you hold no key for are
invited by mail); Add member..., Remove member... and Rename group... work on
the group shown.  Chat > Leave asks first, then deletes the chat and its
messages from this device (a group is left first, and the others are told).
.PP
Chat > Invite... can also mail the link through tvmail (when it is installed) to
someone's ordinary address.  tvmail can hand tvchat a request (Message > Chat with sender, Message > Open
chat invite): one-line files in
.IR ~/.local/state/tvchat/requests/ .
.SH COMMANDS
A line typed in the input line may be a command; the server messages take
nothing else.  The words are the same for IRC, talk and Delta Chat, so the
three feel alike; one that means nothing for the protocol of the conversation
is refused, and stays in the input line.
.B //text
sends a line that starts with
.BR / .
.B /help
lists the commands this account has and
.BI "/help " NAME
explains one (and lists its aliases).
.TP
.B /help \fR[\fICOMMAND\fR]
.TQ
.B /status
this account: its state, who you are, what is open.
.TP
.BI /me " ACTION"
say it in the third person (IRC: a CTCP action; the others send
.IR "* NAME ACTION" ).
.TP
.BR /shrug " [\fITEXT\fP]"
send TEXT followed by a shrug.
.TP
.BR /join " TARGET"
IRC: join a channel (\fI#CHANNEL [KEY]\fR).  Delta Chat: chat with an address (a
first chat mails them an invitation) or join an invite link.  talk: call
someone (\fIUSER[@HOST]\fR).  Alias /j.
.TP
.BR /query " TARGET [\fITEXT\fP]"
open a conversation with someone, and say TEXT (talk: call them).  Alias /q.
.TP
.BI /msg " TARGET TEXT"
send TEXT to someone, opening the conversation (IRC, Delta Chat).  Aliases
/m, /dm.
.TP
.BR /leave " [\fI#CHANNEL\fP] [\fIREASON\fP]"
IRC: leave a channel or close a query; talk: hang up or decline; Delta Chat:
delete this chat from this device (a group is left first; the window asks
before it does).  Aliases /part, /close.
.TP
.BI /invite " TARGET"
IRC: invite a nick to a channel (\fINICK [#CHANNEL]\fR).  Delta Chat: add an
address to this group, or invite it to chat.
.TP
.B /names
list who is in this channel or chat.  Alias /members.
.TP
.BI /whois " TARGET"
IRC: ask the server about someone (the answer is in the server messages).
Delta Chat: what is known about a contact.
.TP
.BI /nick " NAME"
IRC: change your nick.  Delta Chat: change the name people see.
.TP
.BI /name " NEW NAME"
Delta Chat: rename this group.
.TP
.BR /topic " [\fITEXT\fP]"
IRC: set this channel's topic, or show it.
.TP
.BR /away " [\fIREASON\fP]"
IRC: mark yourself away; without a reason, back.
.TP
.BR /ping " [\fINICK\fP]"
IRC: the round-trip time to the server, or to NICK (a CTCP PING).
.TP
.B /clear
clear this conversation's log on screen (PgUp brings older lines back).
.TP
.BR /quit " [\fIREASON\fP]"
disconnect this account.  Alias /exit.
.TP
.BI /quote " LINE"
IRC: send a raw IRC line.  Alias /raw.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--debug " [FILE]
Write diagnostic logging to
.I FILE
(default:
.IR debug.tvchat.log );
.B tvchat\-backend
logs beside it, to
.I debug.tvchat.backend.log
\(em every request, every IRC line in and out (SASL redacted), and why a
connection ended.
.TP
.BI "--trace " [FILE]
Write Chrome Trace Event profiling data (default:
.IR trace.tvchat.log ;
the backend's cProfile data to
.IR trace.tvchat.backend.log ).
.TP
.B --selftest
Start the backend, read the accounts, poll once and ping, without the
interface; exit 0 if all of that worked.
.SH "MESSAGES FROM OTHER USERS"
While tvchat runs, its terminal takes no messages: tvchat clears the
terminal's group\-write bit, as
.B "mesg n"
does, so
.BR talk (1)
requests and
.BR write (1)
or
.BR wall (1)
messages from ordinary users do not land on top of the windows.
The mode goes back when tvchat exits, while a command run from it has
the terminal, and while it is stopped.
Root's messages (\fBwall\fP run by root, shutdown notices) still get
through; press
.B Ctrl\-L
to clear them off the screen.
To keep taking messages, set
.RS
.nf
[ui]
mesg = y
.fi
.RE
in
.IR tvchat.conf .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/tvchat/tvchat.conf
the accounts (see
.BR tvchat\-backend (1));
.B $TVCHAT_CONF
names another file.
.I ~/.config/tvmail/tvchat.conf
is read when it is missing.
tvchat itself reads
.B [ui] mesg
(see
.BR "MESSAGES FROM OTHER USERS" ).
.TP
.IR ~/.netrc ", " ~/.mu-tickets
where the SASL passwords are (or
.BR $TVCHAT_PASS_ \fIACCOUNT\fR).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tvchat\-backend (1),
.BR tvmail (1)
